     "type": "application",
     "title": "Use the Model Responsibly",
     "content": "1. **Use the fable for what it's genuinely good at** — building shared vocabulary and buy-in, especially with people who'd never read an academic paper on team effectiveness but will read, or watch a workshop on, a story.\n\n2. **Supplement it with validated tools.** Look up Amy Edmondson's psychological safety survey items, or Google's own published re:Work materials on Project Aristotle, and use them alongside Lencioni's team assessment from Lesson 10 rather than instead of it.\n\n3. **Audit the structural incentives underneath the interpersonal symptoms.** Before declaring a conflict or accountability problem 'fixed' after an offsite, check whether compensation, reporting lines, or resource competition are quietly still rewarding the old behavior.\n\n4. **Track behavior change, not just felt energy.** A great offsite produces a temporary morale spike almost regardless of content. Track it against the thermometer practice from Lesson 10 over two or three quarters before declaring the intervention successful.\n\n5. **Stay skeptical of any single framework, including this one.** Treat the five-dysfunctions pyramid as a strong starting diagnostic lens, cross-check it against research like Tuckman's stages and Hackman's team-effectiveness conditions, and let the combination guide what you actually do next.\n\n6. **Share the sourcing with your team.** Tell them plainly that the fable is a memorable entry point, not the final word, and that you're pairing it with more rigorously tested research — that honesty models the exact intellectual humility the book itself is trying to teach.",
